# Copy to .env.local, which is gitignored, and set your machine's LAN address.
#
# Not localhost: Expo Go runs on the phone, where localhost is the phone
# itself. Find the address with ipconfig and use the Wi-Fi IPv4 value. It is
# a DHCP lease, so it changes when you rejoin the network.
#
# Port 8010 rather than 8000, which is taken by another project on this
# machine. Only EXPO_PUBLIC_ values are readable by the app, and they are
# inlined into the shipped bundle in plaintext, so never put a secret here.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.10:8010

# Google sign in. Both are OAuth client ids from the Google Cloud console, and
# neither is a secret: a client id for an app ships inside the bundle and is
# readable out of any installed build. What protects an account is the backend
# refusing a token whose audience is not one it was told about, so these have to
# match GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS in backend/.env. A client SECRET never belongs here.
#
# The web client is needed on every platform, the phone included, because it is
# the audience the ID token is minted for on Android. Leave it empty and the
# "Continue with Google" button is simply not drawn.
#
# The iOS client is only read on iOS, and the app config derives the iOS URL
# scheme from it, so there is no fourth value to keep in step.
#
# Android needs no id here at all: that client is matched by package name and
# SHA-1 signing fingerprint, which is why a new signing key means a new Android
# client in the console rather than a new line in this file.
#
# None of this works in Expo Go. The sign in library ships native code, so it
# needs a development build, exactly like the map on Android.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=
